<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koblenz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koblenz is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, at the confluence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moselle">Moselle</a>, a point marked by the Deutsches Eck monument. Founded as the Roman fort of Confluentes, it became a residence of the archbishop-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, and in 1791–1792 it sheltered French émigré princes. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1815 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine-province">Rhine Province</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koblenz.jpg" alt="Koblenz" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Weinandt · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schloss_Stolzenfels_01_Koblenz_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
